---
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import ChartCycling from "../../components/ChartCycling.astro";
import ChartDryYear from "../../components/ChartDryYear.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import datasets from "../../data/datasets.json";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

const dry = datasets.drought_years;
const log = datasets.aquaponic_log;
const SITES = ["S1", "S2", "S3", "S4", "S5"] as const;
const FILES: Record<(typeof SITES)[number], string> = {
  S1: "canberra",
  S2: "alice-springs",
  S3: "brisbane",
  S4: "adelaide",
  S5: "darwin",
};
const sample = log.rows.filter((r) => [1, 8, 16, 24, 25, 30, 38, 45, 46, 53, 60].includes(r.day));
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Datasets"
  description="The troubleshooting data the assignments are marked against. All of it is synthetic, all of it is labelled, and this page says exactly how each set was made."
  wide
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p class="warn">
    <strong>Everything on this page is synthetic.</strong> None of it is a measurement.
    It is generated so that the assignments have data with a diagnosable answer,
    and it is labelled in the page, in the filenames and in the first line of every
    file so that it cannot be mistaken for observation later.
  </p>

  <h2>Aquaponic cycling log</h2>
  <p>{log.what_it_is}</p>
  <p><strong>What it is not:</strong> {log.what_it_is_not}</p>

  <Figure
    caption="Sixty days of a 1,000 L system with a 4 m² grow bed. The shaded band is the fault window; the dashed line is the day the operator intervened."
    basis="synthetic data"
    source="Generated by research/model/make_datasets.py."
  >
    <ChartCycling />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    The first three weeks are an ordinary cycle: ammonia rises as fish are fed and
    falls as the first group of nitrifiers establishes, nitrite rises and falls
    behind it as the second group follows, and nitrate accumulates because nothing
    in the system removes it except the plants.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then something goes wrong, and <strong>the point of the exercise is that the
    symptom and the cause are different variables.</strong> Nitrite climbs again from
    day 25 while ammonia stays low — so the first nitrification step is still
    working and the second is not. Read the carbonate hardness column and the
    reason is there: it has been falling since day one, because nitrification
    consumes alkalinity and nothing replaced it. pH follows it down through 6.4,
    and below that the organisms oxidising nitrite slow markedly.
  </p>
  <p>
    A student who reads only pH and nitrite will diagnose "pH crash" and buffer the
    system, which is what the operator did on day 46 and which does work. A student
    who reads the hardness column will notice that the same thing will happen again
    in seven weeks unless the feed rate and the buffering are reconciled. Both
    answers are defensible; only one of them is an engineering answer.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Selected rows from the sixty-day log. The full file is linked below."
    basis="synthetic data"
  >
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Temp °C</th><th scope="col">pH</th>
          <th scope="col">KH mg/L</th><th scope="col">NH₃ mg/L</th>
          <th scope="col">NO₂ mg/L</th><th scope="col">NO₃ mg/L</th><th scope="col">Note</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          sample.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.day}</th>
              <td>{r.temp_c}</td><td>{r.ph}</td><td>{r.kh_mg_l_caco3}</td>
              <td>{r.ammonia_mg_l}</td><td>{r.nitrite_mg_l}</td><td>{r.nitrate_mg_l}</td>
              <td class="note-cell">{r.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>

  <p>
    <a href={withBase("/data/SYNTHETIC-aquaponic-cycling-log.csv")}>Download the full 60-day log (CSV)</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Dry-year rainfall</h2>
  <p>{dry.what_it_is}</p>
  <p><strong>What it is not:</strong> {dry.what_it_is_not}</p>

  <Figure
    caption="Darwin: the synthetic dry year against the long-record monthly mean. The dry-season months are cut hardest, because that is where a storage design actually fails."
    basis="synthetic data"
    source="Derived from Bureau of Meteorology station 014015 monthly statistics."
  >
    <ChartDryYear site="S5" />
  </Figure>

  <Figure caption="Annual totals, all five sites." basis="synthetic data">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Site</th><th scope="col">BoM station</th>
          <th scope="col">Long-record mean</th><th scope="col">Dry year</th>
          <th scope="col">Share of mean</th><th scope="col">File</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          SITES.map((s) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{dry.sites[s].site}</th>
              <td>{dry.sites[s].station}</td>
              <td>{dry.sites[s].mean_annual_mm} mm</td>
              <td>{dry.sites[s].dry_annual_mm} mm</td>
              <td>{Math.round(dry.sites[s].share_of_mean * 100)} %</td>
              <td><a href={withBase(`/data/SYNTHETIC-dry-year-${FILES[s]}.csv`)}>CSV</a></td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>

  <h3>How to use it, and how not to</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Use it to break your design.</strong> Re-run week 9's tank balance on the
      dry year and name what fails first, in which month.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not tune to it.</strong> A design sized for exactly this year is sized for
      a year that never happened. It is one dry year, not a distribution.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not cite it as evidence about a real climate.</strong> If your capstone
      needs a statement about drought frequency, get it from the Bureau, not from here.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reproducing these files</h2>
  <p>
    Both sets are generated deterministically from a fixed seed, so the same run
    produces the same files. The generator is{" "}
    <code>research/model/make_datasets.py</code> in this site's repository, and the
    climate statistics it derives from are the Bureau of Meteorology long-record
    monthly tables for each site's station.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  td {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .note-cell {
    font-variant-numeric: normal;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .warn {
    padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    border: 1px dashed var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius, 4px);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }
</style>
